TESTING STRATEGY
================
How each part of rvw is verified, what can be tested in isolation and what
cannot, and the manual procedures for the parts that need a real Mac, a real
permission or a real model. README.md is the overview of the system itself.

    util/run_tests.sh                    the whole suite, 217 tests, about 20s
    util/run_tests.sh -k segmenter       one component


The three layers, and why there are three
-----------------------------------------
1. Unit tests over the python modules. Fast, hermetic, and the bulk of the
   suite. Nothing here starts a model, opens a socket to a real endpoint, asks
   for a permission or touches the real archive.
2. Shell function tests over the util/ scripts. The installers and probes are
   sourced for their functions and driven against stand-in commands, so the
   logic in them is tested without running lms, launchctl or tccutil.
3. Manual end to end verification, listed at the end of this file. Some things
   can only be proved by a person: a permission prompt, a hotkey, whether an
   explanation is any good.

The layering is deliberate. Everything that can be pulled down into layer 1 has
been, because layer 3 costs a person twenty minutes and cannot be run in a loop.
The seams that make that possible are named component by component below.


What makes isolation possible
-----------------------------
Four seams do nearly all the work, and any new code should use them rather than
inventing a fifth:

  A helper is a subprocess at a configurable path.  config.capture_helper_path
  and config.screen_capture_helper_path are module level, so a test points them
  at a small stand-in script that prints the metadata a real helper would print,
  or exits non-zero to be the failing case. No Swift, no permission, no window.

  Every path is derived from config.  RVW_ARCHIVE_DIR and RVW_INDEX_DB, and the
  module level variables behind them, are reassigned in setUp to a temporary
  directory. Nothing a test does can reach the real var/meetings.

  Time is a parameter.  RollingTranscript.render_window, screenshot capture and
  the archive all take the current time as an argument rather than reading the
  clock. A test asserts on an exact window boundary instead of sleeping.

  The transcript offers each accepted utterance to a sink.  Retention hangs off
  that one funnel, so the archive can be tested with hand made segments and the
  transcript can be tested with a recording sink, neither needing the other.

  A shell script's functions can be sourced.  Every util/ script guards its
  entry point with [[ ${BASH_SOURCE[0]} == "$0" ]], so sourcing defines the
  functions and does nothing else. test/shell_script_testing.py makes a copy
  truncated at the bare "exit" that ends each script - sourcing that exit would
  end the test's own shell and every assertion would then pass without testing
  anything - inside a stand-in repository whose util/ is a copy and whose other
  directories are symlinks to the real ones, so the script still resolves src/
  and .venv/ above itself. A directory of stand-in commands goes ahead of the
  real ones on PATH, and $HOME is moved, which is how a test drives a script
  that shells out to lms or launchctl without either being installed.


Component by component
----------------------

segmenter - test_segmenter.py, 9 tests
  Pure function of an array of samples, so it is tested with synthesised audio
  and nothing else. What is asserted: speech followed by enough silence closes
  an utterance; silence alone produces none; an over-long utterance is cut at
  the maximum; steady low level noise and a short blip are discarded as noise,
  which is the gate that stops Whisper inventing "Thank you."; speech still
  unclosed at the end of capture is flushed; and the size of the blocks the
  audio arrives in does not change the result, because the timing comes from the
  sample count rather than from a clock reading. This is the component where a
  unit test is worth most, because
  its failures are subtle and never look like errors - they look like a slightly
  wrong transcript.

transcript - test_transcript.py, 10 tests
  In-memory and clock-injected. Window boundaries, chronological interleaving of
  the two streams, pruning at the retention horizon, rejection of a blank
  recognition and of an unknown stream name, and the rendering that the prompts
  are built from. A second class covers the sink contract on its own: every
  accepted utterance is offered exactly once, a blank one and one from an
  unknown stream are not offered at all. That contract is what Phase 3 hangs
  off, so it is asserted from the transcript's side as well as the archive's.

meeting_archive - test_meeting_archive.py, 23 tests
  The property asserted first is the negative one: in ephemeral mode nothing
  whatever reaches the disk, and a session never asked to be kept leaves no
  directory. Then the canonical file: one JSON object per utterance, appended
  and never rewritten, each line carrying its own speaker label and local time;
  transcript.md derived from it and rebuilt from it; metadata.json holding what
  the transcript cannot say about itself. Then the decisions: switching
  retention on is not retrospective, switching it on again appends rather than
  truncating, switching it off leaves what is written, an utterance from an
  unknown stream is fatal rather than a written line, and a damaged transcript
  line stops the reader naming the file and the line number rather than being
  skipped.

meeting_index - test_meeting_index.py, 18 tests
  Built over a synthetic archive of transcript.jsonl files written by the test,
  so it is fast and needs no meeting. The disposability is asserted directly:
  a rebuilt index over the same transcripts gives the same answers, and a
  deleted index comes back. Also: an ephemeral meeting contributes nothing
  because there is no canonical file to authorise; a meeting with "index": false
  in its metadata is excluded; a query full of punctuation is quoted into a safe
  FTS MATCH string rather than surfacing a syntax error; BM25 puts the passage
  matching more of the words first; a hit carries the meeting, the date, the
  passage timestamp, the words and the screenshots captured within
  screenshot_association_seconds of it in the same directory.

recall - test_recall.py, 12 tests
  Formatting only, and therefore trivially isolated: the numbered passages block
  handed to the model, and the source list printed to me, carry the same [n]
  tags, so an answer that cites [2] can be traced to the second source shown. A
  citation names the meeting, the date and the time, because a reference that
  cannot be found is not a reference.

prompts - test_prompts.py, 16 tests
  The prompts are data, so the tests assert their shape rather than their taste:
  an empty transcript window raises instead of asking the model about nothing, a
  missing image raises, the transcript framing is present, and the messages have
  the roles and order the endpoint expects. Whether a prompt produces a good
  answer is not a unit test; that is the bench and the manual runs below.

llm - test_llm.py, 11 tests
  Driven against a stub HTTP layer, so no endpoint is needed. Both guards
  against the endpoint's model substitution are tested separately, because
  either alone can be defeated: the check that the identifier is served before
  anything is asked, and the check of the answering model named in each streamed
  chunk. Also: reasoning tokens are kept out of the answer and returned only
  through the reasoning callback; an answer that arrived only on the reasoning
  channel is still returned rather than lost; the empty-<think> prefill is
  appended for the text model and not for the vision model.

model_loader - test_model_loader.py, 8 tests
  Loading a model really costs minutes and twenty gigabytes, so nothing here
  runs lms: run_lms is a module level function precisely so a test can stand in
  for it and record the command that would have been run. Asserted: a model
  already served is not loaded again; an unloaded one is, with the identifier,
  context length and idle timeout from config; the lms model key is resolved
  from the listing rather than guessed; a failing lms becomes a ModelLoadError
  naming the installer.

config - test_config.py, 9 tests
  One setting differs per machine and it decides whether the assistant is usable
  at all, so it is tested as a function rather than read as a constant:
  hostname normalisation (m4, m4.local and m4.lan are one machine), the per host
  table, the default for a machine not in it, and RVW_LLM_SOURCE_MODEL
  overriding - with set-but-empty counting as saying nothing, because that is
  how an unset variable arrives from a launchd plist.

commands - test_commands.py, 8 tests
  The dispatcher is transport independent, so it is tested with no transport:
  registration, case insensitivity, argument splitting, the OK/FAIL reply
  shape, an unknown command listing the known ones, and a handler that raises
  becoming a FAIL line rather than taking the daemon down.

screenshot - test_screenshot.py, 10 tests
  The Swift helper needs a permission and a real window, so the python side is
  driven against a stand-in helper script at config.screen_capture_helper_path.
  Asserted: where the image lands in the session directory, the millisecond
  naming that lets a burst not collide, the sidecar JSON merging what the helper
  saw with what only the assistant knows, and a failing capture being reported
  rather than hidden.

audio_source - test_audio_source.py, 3 tests
  The same stand-in trick for the capture helper. The case that matters is the
  one a real permission failure produces: a helper that exits immediately must
  make start() raise, so the command that started it reports FAIL instead of
  answering OK about a stream that is not running. A missing helper binary names
  helper/build.sh.

assistant - test_assistant.py, 32 tests
  Command wiring and the states the commands leave behind, not the quality of an
  answer: every command up to Phase 3 is registered; an ephemeral session says
  so and never writes the speech it hears; retention writes what follows it and
  not what preceded it; SCREENSHOT reports where the image went and calls no
  model at all; INTERPRET_SCREEN still reports the archived image when no vision
  model is loaded, and asks no other model to stand in for it; UNGARBLE without
  speech fails instead of asking the model nonsense; SEARCH and RECALL refuse an
  empty query, say so plainly when nothing matches, and RECALL never calls the
  model when there is nothing to ground it on. Capture, recognition and the
  model client are covered in their own tests or need real hardware.

app bundle - test_app_bundle.py, 12 tests
  bin/rvw.app is what owns the macOS permissions, and its permanence is fragile:
  an ad hoc signature is pinned to the exact bytes of the launcher, so a rebuild
  voids every grant. Asserted: the launcher is where the Info.plist says it is,
  the bundle identifies itself as a background application, every permission the
  assistant needs has a usage description, the signature verifies, and -- the
  point of the file -- rebuilding leaves the signature alone so the grants
  survive. A second class covers the launcher's own contract: it runs the named
  program from bin/, passes the remaining arguments through, records the pid and
  the exit status in a log it empties at each launch, and refuses a program name
  that escapes the bin directory.

permission probes - test_permission_probe.py, 7 tests
  The probes are the only source of truth about the permissions, because there
  is no API that answers the question, so a probe that lies is worse than no
  probe. These exist because of a real lie that lasted days: both audio probes
  read var/log/audio_capture.launcher.log, the launcher outlives its helper by
  the moment it takes to record the exit, and the previous launcher's exit line
  landed in the log the next probe had just emptied, so whichever audio probe
  ran second always reported its permission missing. The tests drive the sourced
  probe functions against stand-in logs: sourcing the script probes nothing and
  says nothing while executing it still runs the probes; the success phrase and
  a refusal each end the wait at once; stopping a helper waits until its
  launcher has reported the exit, and a helper that had already exited needs no
  waiting. Anything that reads a log another process is still writing needs the
  same test.

start_llm_server - test_start_llm_server.py, 8 tests
  The properties that only fail after a reboot, which is why they are tested
  rather than tried by hand: lms is invoked by the absolute path from config and
  not found on PATH - a decoy lms on PATH proves it, because a bare lms works in
  every hand test and is missing at the one moment that matters; a running
  daemon and a server already on the configured port are left alone, because
  the script runs at every login; a stopped one is started, and a server
  listening on the wrong port is moved to the configured one. A missing lms says
  how to install it, and dry mode starts nothing.

init_llm_autostart - test_init_llm_autostart.py, 9 tests
  The agent's only failure mode is silence, so the generated plist is parsed
  rather than matched as text: it is written where launchd looks for it, it runs
  the start script of this checkout, it runs at login and is not kept alive, its
  output goes to the repository log directory, and it is not left writable by
  anyone else. Installing it again changes nothing and booting out an agent that
  was never loaded is not a failure, because util/init.sh reruns the installer
  every time. Dry mode writes no plist and calls no launchctl.

list_models - test_list_models.py, 12 tests
  Driven against stand-in lms and a stand-in Hugging Face cache. Both model
  stores are listed, because a listing that showed only the LM Studio one would
  look complete without being complete, and an absent identifier is reported
  INFO in exactly the words the running assistant uses for the same state. The
  one genuine failure is an endpoint serving nothing.


What has no unit test, and why
------------------------------
  helper/*.swift          Core Audio, AVAudioEngine and ScreenCaptureKit against
                          a real permission and a real window. Covered by the
                          manual procedures below and by the permission probes.
  asr.py                  a thin wrapper over mlx_whisper; a test would assert
                          that the library was called, which is worth nothing.
                          Its real verification is the warm-up timing in the
                          start-up log and the manual recognition run.
  recognizer.py           a queue and a thread around asr and transcript, both
                          of which are tested. Worth a test if it grows logic.
  control.py              a unix socket around the dispatcher, which is tested.
                          Exercised for real by every bin/rvwctl command.
  session_log.py          logging configuration.
  init_local_models.sh    it downloads twenty gigabytes and builds a venv; the
                          settings it reads out of config.py are tested through
                          assistant_settings.sh in the other script tests.
  bin/rvwctl              stdlib-only client, exercised by every manual step.
  rvw_hotkeys.lua         needs Hammerspoon and a real key press.


Manual verification
-------------------
Run these after a change to the helpers, the permissions, the bundle or the
model choice - and note the result in the relevant doc/phase file, which is
where the previous runs are recorded.

  Permissions
    util/init_permissions.sh
    Expect all four granted. Run it twice: the second run catches a probe
    reading the previous probe's verdict, which is the bug that hid for days.

  Screen capture
    bin/screen_capture --output /tmp/shot.png --target frontmost
    Three times in quick succession from the assistant, and check that three
    PNGs with distinct millisecond names and three complete sidecar JSON files
    landed in the session directory.

  Recognition, both streams
    bin/rvw --source both --listen, then say something into the microphone and
    play speech through the speakers ("say" is enough). Expect two labelled
    streams, "me" and "them", within about ten seconds of speaking. The
    microphone as a transcript stream is the one still unverified on the M4.

  EXPLAIN and UNGARBLE
    Play a passage with some jargon in it, press alt-cmd-E, then alt-cmd-C.
    Judge the answer, and time it: the model sizing decision lives on those
    seconds. UNGARBLE should be terse and in its fixed three-part shape; if it
    rambles, the prompt has stopped holding the model, which is the failure mode
    of a model whose thinking channel is closed.

  Retention
    An ephemeral session must leave var/meetings untouched - check that first.
    Then alt-cmd-T, speak, QUIT, and check transcript.jsonl, metadata.json and
    the rendered transcript.md.

  Search and recall
    util/rebuild_index.sh, then bin/rvwctl SEARCH <words> and bin/rvwctl RECALL
    <question>. Delete var/index/meetings.db, rebuild, and confirm the same
    answers: that is the property that makes the index disposable. Check that
    each printed source can actually be opened.

  Hotkeys
    Every key in hammerspoon/rvw_hotkeys.lua, actually pressed. A command sent
    with bin/rvwctl reaches the same dispatcher entry but proves nothing about
    the binding. The two screenshot keys must show nothing on success.

  A model, before it is chosen
    util/bench_model.sh <served-model-id>, and append the row to
    doc/model_benchmarks.md. Never delete a row: a model that failed on a
    machine is exactly what stops us trying it again. Add a machine to
    config.llm_source_model_by_host only once it has rows there.


Writing a new test
------------------
The project is test first: write the failing test, make it pass, then refactor.
Three habits keep the suite worth having.

Reach for the seams above rather than for a mock of the standard library. If a
new component cannot be tested without one, the component probably needs the
same treatment run_lms got: pull the expensive call out into a module level
function the test can stand in for.

Assert the negative properties, not only the positive ones. The tests that have
earned their keep here are the ones asserting that an ephemeral session writes
nothing, that a substituted model is refused, that a probe does not read another
probe's verdict, and that build_app.sh does nothing when nothing changed.

Treat a violated assumption as fatal in the test as in the code. A transcript
line that cannot be parsed stops the rebuild; the test asserts that it stops,
not that it is skipped.
